{
	title: 'Web tab bar and list sheet: card-tinted header, rail and status dots',
	spec: "On phones and narrow panes (<= 40rem), the web tab bar is a two-tier console: a card-tinted title header (3px left project LED, dark project tint, project-colored number, bright readable name and status indicator) beside the list/count button on row 1, over a full-width natively scrolling number rail on row 2. Wider viewports fold into one 36px row where the shown tab uses the same card-tinted face and left LED. Rail tabs show neutral numbers, a bottom project LED bar (2px inset, 3px full-width when active on phones), and a separate 6px round status dot in the top-right corner (inline on desktop active cards) colored from the host theme's [data-mark] rules: solid while waiting, hollow while paused, smoothly pulsing in theme colors (including jobs color) while working. No solid neon fills, rings over numbers, or blinking squares. Touch targets stay at least 44px on coarse pointers. The count button opens the tab list sheet and shows an inline dot for the most urgent waiting mark among tabs under 10% visible. The new-tab button shows on fine pointers. Selecting a tab centers it on the nearest tab start; scrolling never selects. The tab list sheet has a compact header with total count and close button, rows with a 3px left project LED, aligned numbers, a dedicated status-dot column, project-tinted active row (never status green), a 2x2 primary action grid (New tab, Find, Notifications, Reload page), and a compact utility row for diagnostics and touch debug. Replaces task ce's chevrons, task 3k's web menu button and equal web cells, task gn's tinted current cell and task fr's web glyphs; the terminal is unchanged.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"The shown tab is wider than the others, so the visible range comes from each tab's rendered rect, and centering targets the tab start nearest the ideal center: proximity snapping lands there anyway, and an exact center would snap back. The track is position: relative so offsetLeft is track-relative.",
		'Mark colors moved from .marker classes to [data-mark] on the tab link (host/web-page.ts), so a ring, a blink and the count dot share one theme rule.',
		"Design: mockup approved by the user (2026-11-23), session 54-dot. Rings use the theme's terminal mark colors (done and asking green, approval and blocked yellow, failed red, paused gray).",
